1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:08,279
Accounting now runs on AI, automation, and blockchain, turning record keepers into analysts and advisors.

2
00:00:09,329 --> 00:00:13,370
So, does learning with technology build more than technical skills?

3
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:20,375
Employers see a gap between the skills they need and what graduates bring.

4
00:00:20,725 --> 00:00:28,485
Programs lean on theory with little hands-on practice, and past studies haven't shown how technology builds competencies.

5
00:00:29,535 --> 00:00:35,753
Kolb says we learn by doing, Siemens through digital networks, and Bandura by observing others.

6
00:00:36,803 --> 00:00:42,991
An Input, Process, Output model links respondents and competencies to recommendations.

7
00:00:44,041 --> 00:00:50,908
We measured five competencies: intellectual, technical, personal, interpersonal, and organizational.

8
00:00:52,758 --> 00:00:56,813
Our null hypothesis: there is no significant relationship.

9
00:00:57,863 --> 00:01:04,009
In a mixed-methods design, a survey measured the relationship, and interviews explained why.

10
00:01:05,059 --> 00:01:12,020
From 112 newly passed CPAs from PLV, Slovin's formula set a sample of 88.

11
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:18,937
They rated each statement on a four-point Likert scale, and eight joined in-depth interviews.

12
00:01:19,987 --> 00:01:24,641
We used weighted means, Pearson correlation, and thematic analysis.

13
00:01:25,691 --> 00:01:30,183
Participation was voluntary, and data stays protected by law.

14
00:01:30,633 --> 00:01:35,181
So, what did we find? 88 newly passed CPAs answered the survey.

15
00:01:35,331 --> 00:01:41,649
Almost all are employed, most in private or corporate accounting, with two years of experience or less.

16
00:01:42,099 --> 00:01:45,960
All five competencies scored high, on a scale of 1 to 4.

17
00:01:46,110 --> 00:01:53,287
Intellectual skills ranked highest, at 3.31, led by finding information from digital resources.

18
00:01:53,437 --> 00:02:00,586
Technical, personal, and organizational scored 3.26; interpersonal, 3.09 (lowest).

19
00:02:01,536 --> 00:02:06,861
Exposure to technology was rated positive overall, at 3.00,

20
00:02:07,011 --> 00:02:11,550
but hands-on accounting software and collaborative tools scored the lowest.

21
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:20,267
Pearson correlation found a strong, significant relationship: r = .67, p < .001.

22
00:02:20,417 --> 00:02:25,795
So the null hypothesis is rejected. About 44% of the variance is shared (r² = .44),

23
00:02:25,945 --> 00:02:28,955
though this shows an association, not proof of cause.

24
00:02:30,005 --> 00:02:35,275
The interviews explained why: technology made studying faster and more independent,

25
00:02:35,425 --> 00:02:39,753
and internships showed how classroom tools work in real accounting jobs.

26
00:02:40,203 --> 00:02:44,273
We recommend that students explore digital tools beyond the classroom,

27
00:02:44,423 --> 00:02:48,856
educators use spreadsheets, simulations, and case-based activities,

28
00:02:49,006 --> 00:02:54,121
and PLV invest in accounting software, computer labs, and faculty training.

29
00:02:54,871 --> 00:02:58,816
Because the accountant of the future needs more than technical skills.

